WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Safer ABI design often starts by hiding what does not need to be part of the binary contract.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.640
Opaque handles let callers refer to an object without knowing its layout.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:16.380
A C-compatible facade can reduce C++ ABI complexity at plugin, SDK, or cross-compiler boundaries.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:23.440
PIMPL can keep source-facing C++ ergonomics while moving private implementation details out of the public object layout.

5
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.520
Versioned structs are another useful pattern.

6
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:33.180
A structure can include a size or version field so newer libraries can understand what the caller knows how to provide.

7
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:40.800
Capability queries let callers and libraries negotiate supported behavior rather than assuming that every feature exists.

8
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.460
These patterns make evolution deliberate.

9
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.160
They are not magic, but they give the interface a place to express compatibility rather than relying on guesswork.

10
00:00:49.440 --> 00:01:00.400
Boundary contracts should define ownership, allocation, deallocation, error reporting, thread-safety expectations, lifetime rules, and compatibility policy.

11
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.780
A narrow exported API is easier to test, document, and preserve.

12
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.680
The goal is not to make every boundary look like C.

13
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:14.860
The goal is to keep the binary contract stable enough that implementation details can change safely behind it.

14
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.160
Defensive design gives future releases room to evolve without surprising existing callers.

15
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.460
A good boundary also makes unsupported use fail clearly instead of becoming accidental coupling.

16
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.700
These patterns work best when they are paired with examples and negative space.

17
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:42.160
The documentation should show the supported creation, use, and destruction sequence, but it should also say which casts, direct structure access, retained pointers, or cross-version assumptions are outside the contract.

18
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.220
Clear limits reduce accidental dependencies and make compatibility testing more meaningful.
